      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "There is a need for controlled trials among children with asthma to evaluate and compare different markers of inflammation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Our goal was to investigate the effect of withdrawal of inhaled budesonide on repeated measurements of exhaled NO (ENO), peripheral blood eosinophils (PBE), sputum/NAL/serum-eosinophil cationic protein (ECP), bronchial hyperresponsiveness (BHR) and forced expiratory volume in 1 s (FEV(1)) in children with allergic asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Eighteen asthmatic children were randomly allocated to continue or discontinue use of inhaled budesonide. They were followed up, at six visits for 4 months with regular blood, serum, sputum, and NAL samples. Sixteen age-matched healthy children served as controls."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "ENO, PBE, and S-ECP increased significantly in the withdrawal group (p < 0.05) but not in the continuous treatment group. No trend could be observed during the study for markers in sputum or in NAL in either group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The present data provide evidence for the clinical usefulness of measuring ENO, PBE, and S-ECP and when combined they could help to avoid over- and undertreatment with corticosteroids in the growing child."
        }
      ]},
